@node Top
@top Classy

  Feature overview:

  @itemize
  @item @emph{Cluster management}:
  sites can join together to form clusters or be removed from the cluster.
  Peer information is propagated between the peers using gossip protocol.

  @item @emph{Persistent node identities}:
  nodes are assigned persistent identities that survive hostname changes.

  @item @emph{Service discovery}:
  sites can automatically discover one another using one of the cluster discovery strategies.

  @item @emph{Automatic cleanup}:
  sites that remain down for longer than @ref{max_site_downtime} are automatically removed.

  @item @emph{Persistence layer}:
  classy implements a standalone persistent table similar to mnesia @code{local_content} @code{disk_copies} table
  that can be used by third-party applications:
  @erlmodref{ref,classy_table}

  @item @emph{Unique ID generation}:
  @erlmodref{ref,classy_uid}

  @item @emph{Two-Phase Commit}:
  @erlmodref{ref,classy_vote}

  @item @emph{Boot management}:
  classy can orchestrate startup and shutdown of business applications.

  @end itemize

@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

  @node Concepts
  @section Concepts
  @cindex Site ID
  @anchor{Site ID}
  @b{Site ID} is a random unique identifier of the node that persists between restarts and hostname changes.
  Each node creates this identifier once and then never changes it.

  By default, classy initializes site with a random value.
  Business applications can override this behavior by registering a @erlfn{link,erlref,classy,on_node_init,2} hook
  containing a call to @erlfn{link,erlref,classy_node,maybe_init_the_site,1}:

  @example erlang
  classy:on_node_init(
    fun() ->
       classy_node:maybe_init_the_site(MySiteId)
    end,
    0)
  @end example

  @cindex Cluster ID
  @anchor{Cluster ID}
  @b{Cluster ID} is a unique random identifier of the cluster.

  Each site starts in its very own cluster with a random ID.
  When a site joins a remote node,
  it inherits the cluster ID of the remote.

  @cindex Run level
  @anchor{Run level}
  @b{Run level} is a global system state that indicates ``readiness'' of the local site.
  It is represented by an integer in the range @code{0..300}
  and is derived from the configuration and the number of peers.

  The run levels are split into the following ranges:

  @enumerate
    @item @code{stopped} (@code{0..9}):
    Classy application itself is not running or is not ready.
    These run levels are reserved for classy's own initialization logic,
    business applications must not use them.

    @item @code{single} (@code{10..99}):
    Classy application is ready to process cluster membership information,
    but the total number of @emph{known} sites (including the local site)
    may be less than @ref{n_sites}.
    These run levels correspond to initialization of a singleton node.

    @item @code{cluster} (@code{100..199}):
    the total number of @emph{known} sites (including the local site) is at least @ref{n_sites},
    but the number of @emph{connected} sites (including the local site) may be less than @ref{quorum}.

    @item @code{quorum} (@code{200..299}):
    the number of @emph{connected} sites (including the local site) satisfies the @ref{quorum} configuration.

    @item @code{ready} (@code{300}):
    final run level.
    The site is fully connected and operational.
  @end enumerate

  Unless the whole BEAM VM or the classy application is stopped abruptly
  (@code{erlang:halt}, @code{kill}, power loss),
  the run level is stepped in an increasing or decreasing arithmetic progression:
  it always advances and regresses in sequence.
  When @erlfn{ref,erlref,classy,start_system,0} is called for the first time,
  the system starts at the @code{stopped} run level (@code{0})
  and eventually progresses to the @code{ready} run level (@code{300}).
  Effect of @erlfn{ref,erlref,classy,stop_system,0} is the opposite.

  Business applications can track the run level via @erlfn{link,erlref,classy,on_run_level,2} hook.
  This hook can, for example,
  activate or deactivate some business applications.

  The boot sequence and @emph{barriers} that can pause the run level progression
  are managed by the @erlmodref{ref,classy_boot} module.

  @node Network Partitions
  @section Network Partitions

  Classy guarantees that all cluster members will eventually converge to the same state,
  but earlier join and leave commands @emph{may} override later commands.

  These adverse side effects can be observed when conflicting commands are issued on different nodes faster than the nodes sync with each other.
  This is most likely to happen during a network partition.

@node Autocluster
@chapter Autocluster

  @cindex Peer discovery
  Autocluster is a mechanism for automatic peer discovery.
  Peer discovery is done with the help of several modules implementing
  @erlmodref{link,classy_discovery_strategy} behavior.

  The strategy is configured via @ref{discovery_strategy} OTP application environment variable.
  This variable has the form of a 2-tuple with the following elements:

  @enumerate
    @item Discovery method, such as @code{static}
    @item Method-specific parameters
  @end enumerate

  New methods can be registered using @erlfn{ref,erlref,classy_discovery_strategy,hook,2} function.

  Additionally, there is a hook that can be used to filter and prioritize discovered nodes:
  @erlfn{ref,erlref,classy,pre_autocluster,2}.

  Autocluster logic runs only on singleton sites.
  It is inactive on sites that already have peers (up or down).

  The following strategies are available:

  @node Manual
  @section Manual
    ``Manual'' strategy disables automatic peer discovery.
    When it is selected,
    the site simply waits until the operator manually calls @erlfn{ref,erlref,classy,join_node,2}.

    Example configuration:

    @example
    @{manual, []@}
    @end example

  @node Static
  @section Static
     Join one of the nodes explicitly pre-configured in the list.

     Example configuration:

     @example
     @{static, #@{seeds => ['foo@@example.com', 'bar@@example.com']@}@}
     @end example

  @node DNS
  @section DNS
    Discover peers via DNS query.

    Configuration parameters:

    @table @code
      @item name
      Type: @code{string()} (Required).

      Domain name.

      @item type
      Type: @code{a | aaaa | srv}, default: @code{a}.

      DNS record type.

      @item app
      Type: @code{string() | atom()}, default: @erlfn{ref,erlref,classy_autocluster,app_name,0}.

      Node name prefix.
    @end table

    Node names are derived using the following template @code{App@@Hostname},
    where @code{App} is the value of @code{app} configuration option,
    and @code{Hostname} is derived from the DNS response.

    When @code{a} or @code{aaaa} record type is used, hostnames become IP addresses.
    It is recommended to use @code{srv} records.

  @node K8s
  @section K8s

    This discovery strategy relies on the Kubernetes API server.
    It queries the Kubernetes API endpoint @code{/api/v1/namespaces/@{namespace@}/endpoints/@{service_name@}}
    to retrieve the IP addresses or hostnames of all pods associated with that service,
    which are then converted into Erlang node names.

    Configuration parameters:
    @table @code
      @item apiserver
      Type: @code{string()} (Required).

      The URL of the Kubernetes API server.

      @item service_name
      Type: @code{string()} (Required).

      The name of the Kubernetes Service used for discovery.

      @item app_name
      Type: @code{string()}, default: @erlfn{ref,erlref,classy_autocluster,app_name,0}.

      The application name used as a prefix for the generated node names.

      @item address_type
      Type: @code{ip | hostname | dns}, default: @code{ip}.

      Determines the address extraction and node naming format.

      @item namespace
      Type: @code{string()}, default: @code{"default"}.

      The Kubernetes namespace where the service is located.

      @item suffix
      Type: @code{string()}, default: @code{""}.

      An optional DNS suffix appended to the node name.
      Only used with the @code{hostname} and @code{dns} address types.
    @end table

  @node etcd
  @section etcd

    Discover peers by querying etcd service.

    Configuration parameters:

    @table @code
      @item server
      Type: @code{[string()]} (Required).

      List of etcd server addresses to connect to.

      @item prefix
      Type: @code{string()} (Required).

      Key prefix to use for service discovery.

      @item version
      Type: @code{v2 | v3}, default: @code{v3}.

      Version of the etcd API to use.

      @item ssl_options
      Type: @code{[proplists:property()]}, default: @code{[]}.

      TLS options.
      TLS is disabled when the list is empty,
      or when it contains @code{@{enable, false@}}.

      @item cluster_name
      Type: @code{string()}.

      Cluster name used as a part of the etcd key path.
      Only used by the @code{v2} API version.

      @item node_ttl
      Type: @code{pos_integer()}.

      TTL of the etcd key holding the node information, in seconds.
      Only used by the @code{v2} API version.
    @end table

@node Configuration
@chapter Configuration

  @code{classy} is configured via OTP application environment variables and callbacks.

@macro defenv{NAME, TYPE}
@anchor{\NAME\}
@unnumberedsubsec \NAME\
@vindex \NAME\
@emph{Type}: \TYPE\
@end macro

@macro defen{NAME, TYPE, DEFAULT}
@defenv{\NAME\, \TYPE\}

@emph{Default}: \DEFAULT\
@end macro

  @section General
    @defenv{setup_hooks, @erltp{ref,erlref,classy_lib,mfargs,0}}

      A callback that classy executes during startup.
      It allows business applications to set up other hooks using a more type-safe API.

    @defen{table_dir, @code{file:filename()}, @code{"."}}

      Directory where persistent data is stored.
      It should be writable.

    @defen{table_batch_size, @code{pos_integer()}, @code{100}}

      Number of records processed in a single batch
      when the persistence layer reads the write-ahead log or compacts a table.

    @defen{rpc_timeout, @code{timeout()}, @code{5_000}}

      @emph{Unit}: ms

      Default timeout for remote procedure calls.

    @defen{hook_timeout, @code{timeout()}, @code{30_000}}

      @emph{Unit}: ms

      Maximum run time for any hook.

    @defen{rand_id_bytes, @code{pos_integer()}, @code{16}}

      Number of bytes used in random identifiers,
      such as site IDs and cluster IDs.

      WARNING: this value should be sufficiently large to account for the birthday problem.
      Note that new cluster IDs are created every time a node leaves a cluster,
      so the number of unique random IDs is not limited to the number of sites.

    @defen{boot_diagnostic_interval, @code{pos_integer()}, @code{15_000}}

      @emph{Unit}: ms

       @erlmodref{ref,classy_boot} module can print the boot state of the node to help diagnose problems.
       This parameter specifies how often the diagnostics are printed.

       NOTE: the message is suppressed when the node is fully booted.

  @section Peer

    @defen{sync_timeout, @code{non_neg_integer()}, @code{1_000}}

      @emph{Unit}: ms

      Maximum interval of time that can pass between the membership CRDT server receiving an update
      and the moment it propagates it to the peers.

    @defen{n_sites, @code{non_neg_integer()}, @code{1}}

      Minimum total number of known sites (including the local site)
      necessary to advance the run level from @code{single} to @code{cluster}.

    @defen{to_cluster_sets, @code{[classy:node_set_name()]}, @code{[]}}

      List of node set names used to additionally restrict when the node is considered joined to a cluster.
      Sets from the lists are combined using set intersection operation.

    @defen{quorum_sets, @code{[classy:node_set_name()]}, @code{[]}}

      List of node set names used to additionally restrict when the node is considered having a quorum.
      Sets from the lists are combined using set intersection operation.

    @defen{quorum, @code{non_neg_integer() | auto}, @code{1}}

      Minimum number of @emph{connected} sites (including the local site)
      necessary to make quorum decisions and to advance the run level from @code{cluster} to @code{quorum}.

      If set to @code{auto},
      then the value is calculated as the majority of @ref{n_sites}.

    @defen{join_lock_autoclean, @code{pos_integer()}, 5m}

      @emph{Unit}: ms

      The default value is 5 minutes.

      When node A joins B, it prevents B from joining other nodes until the procedure is complete.
      This mechanism is mostly used so the initial cluster formation (when multiple nodes join to each other) happens in an orderly fashion.
      However, if node A becomes unresponsive while holding the lock, the lock will automatically expire after set time.
      Tuning this value may be necessary if the join procedure can take a very long time,
      e.g. due to heavy work done in the hooks.

    @defen{force_intents, @code{list()}, @code{[]}}

      List of @erltp{ref,erlref,classy,join_intent,0} atoms that allow overriding join locks.
      When join command is issued with an intent that belongs in the list,
      the local node @emph{A} will proceed to join another node @emph{B}
      even if the third node @emph{C} is trying to join @emph{A}.

  @section Two-phase commit
    @defen{vote_retry_interval, @code{pos_integer()}, @code{5_000}}

      @emph{Unit}: ms

      2PC coordinator re-sends outcome of a transaction to the participants at this interval.

  @section Autoclean
    @defen{max_site_downtime, @code{pos_integer() | infinity}, @code{infinity}}

      @emph{Unit}: s.

      Automatically kick sites that have been down longer than this value from the cluster.

      Note: to prevent network-isolated nodes from kicking their peers,
      quorum among the running nodes is required to perform the act.

    @defen{cleanup_check_interval, @code{pos_integer()}, @code{5_000}}

      @emph{Unit}: ms.

      Autoclean check interval.

    @defen{forget_after, @code{pos_integer()}, @code{604_800}}

      @emph{Unit}: s

      The default value is 1 week.

      Forget information about inactive (kicked) sites after this period of time.

      Note: cleanup procedure may lead to the following situation:

      @enumerate
      @item Site A goes down
      @item Site B kicks A
      @item Information about event 2 propagates throughout the cluster.
      @item Cleanup. All active peers delete data about A.
      @item A goes back up
      @end enumerate

      Since at step 4 we removed the data about event 2, A will reappear in the cluster.

      So @code{forget_after} should be set to a fairly large value to make sure it doesn't cover nodes that can go back online.

  @section Autocluster
    @defen{discovery_interval, @code{pos_integer()}, @code{5_000}}

      @emph{Unit}: ms.

      Peer discovery retry interval.

    @defenv{discovery_strategy, @erltp{ref,erlref,classy_discovery_strategy,t,0}}

      Peer discovery method, @xref{Autocluster}.

      @emph{Default}: @code{@{manual, []@}}

    @defen{discovery_complete_sets, @code{[classy:node_set_name()]}, @code{[]}}

      Consider cluster discovery complete when the intersection of the site sets specified by this parameter,
      with the local site subtracted,
      contains at least one item.

